Sure! Here's an example program that demonstrates the differences between classes and structs in C#:
```
using System;
class Program
{
    static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        // Create a class instance
        MyClass myClass = new MyClass();
        myClass.x = 10;
        myClass.y = 20;
        // Create a struct instance
        MyStruct myStruct = new MyStruct();
        myStruct.x = 10;
        myStruct.y = 20;
        // Print the values of the class and struct instances
        Console.WriteLine($"Class: x={myClass.x}, y={myClass.y}");
        Console.WriteLine($"Struct: x={myStruct.x}, y={myStruct.y}");
    }
}
class MyClass
{
    public int x;
    public int y;
}
struct MyStruct
{
    public int x;
    public int y;
}
```
In this program, we define a class `MyClass` and a struct `MyStruct`, both of which have two integer fields `x` and `y`. In the `Main` method, we create instances of both types and set their `x` and `y` values to 10 and 20. We then print out the values of these instances.
The main difference between classes and structs in C# is how they are stored in memory. Classes are reference types, which means that when you create an instance of a class, a reference to the instance is created on the heap, and the actual data is stored elsewhere in memory. In contrast, structs are value types, which means that when you create an instance of a struct, the data is stored directly in the memory location where the variable is declared.
This difference in memory allocation has implications for efficiency and performance. Because classes are stored on the heap, they require more memory and can be slower to access than structs. However, classes can be more efficient when dealing with large amounts of data, because copying a reference to an instance is faster than copying the entire instance. Additionally, classes can be more flexible than structs, because they can be inherited and can implement interfaces.
To measure the memory usage and performance of your program, you can use the `time` and `dotnet` commands in the shell. The `time` command can be used to measure the execution time of your program, like this:
```
time dotnet run
```
This will run your program and print out the execution time. To measure the memory usage of your program, you can use the `dotnet` command with the `trace` option, like this:
```
dotnet trace collect --process-id <process-id>
```
This will start collecting memory usage data for your program. After your program has finished running, you can stop the trace and view the results like this:
```
dotnet trace collect --process-id <process-id> --stop
dotnet trace analyze
```
This will print out a summary of the memory usage data for your program.

