for i in range(len(arr)):
    sum += arr[i]
print("Sum of all elements: ", sum)
```
```c++
int arr[1000];
int sum = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {
    sum += arr[i];
}
cout << "Sum of all elements: " << sum << endl;
```
Method 2: Using the Arrays.stream() method (Java 8+)
```java
int[] arr = new int[1000];
int sum = Arrays.stream(arr).sum();
System.out.println("Sum of all elements: " + sum);
```
Method 3: Using the accumulate() method from the functools module (Python 3+)
```python
from functools import reduce
arr = [0]*1000
sum = reduce(lambda x, y: x + y, arr)
print("Sum of all elements: ", sum)
```
Note: The C++ code does not have an equivalent built-in method like Java 8's Arrays.stream() or Python's functools.reduce(), but it is still possible to use libraries like the C++ Standard Template Library (STL) to achieve similar functionality.

