To properly torque the lug nuts and avoid over-tightening and damaging the wheel studs, follow these steps:
1. Use a torque wrench to tighten the lug nuts to the manufacturer's recommended torque specification, which can be found in the owner's manual or on the driver's side door jamb.
2. Tighten the lug nuts in a star pattern, starting with one nut and then tightening the nut opposite to it, continuing in a star pattern until all the nuts are tightened.
Here is a sample Java code for aligning the spare tire with the wheel studs and tightening the lug nuts in a star pattern:
```
import java.util.Scanner;
public class SpareTireAlignment {
   public static void main(String[] args) {
      Scanner input = new Scanner(System.in);
      System.out.println("Enter the number of lug nuts: ");
      int numLugNuts = input.nextInt();
      System.out.println("Enter the torque specification: ");
      int torqueSpec = input.nextInt();
      int[] lugNuts = new int[numLugNuts];
      for (int i = 0; i < numLugNuts; i++) {
         System.out.println("Enter the lug nut position: ");
         lugNuts[i] = input.nextInt();
      }
      for (int i = 0; i < numLugNuts; i++) {
         System.out.println("Align the spare tire with the wheel stud at position " + lugNuts[i]);
         System.out.println("Tighten the lug nut at position " + lugNuts[i] + " to " + torqueSpec + " ft-lbs.");
         for (int j = 0; j < numLugNuts; j++) {
            if (j != i && j != (i + numLugNuts/2) % numLugNuts) {
               System.out.println("Tighten the lug nut at position " + lugNuts[j] + " to " + torqueSpec + " ft-lbs.");
            }
         }
      }
   }
}
```
This code prompts the user for the number of lug nuts, the torque specification, and the position of each lug nut. It then aligns the spare tire with each wheel stud and tightens the lug nuts in a star pattern, skipping the opposite lug nut to ensure even distribution of pressure.

Certainly! The MATLAB function for computing the Fourier Transform of a signal x(t) is `fft`. Here's a code snippet that shows how to use it:
```
% Define the time variable
t = 0:0.1:10;
% Define the signal
x = sin(2*pi*5*t) + 0.5*sin(2*pi*10*t);
% Compute the Fourier Transform
X = fft(x);
% Compute the frequency vector
Fs = 1/(t(2)-t(1)); % Sampling frequency
f = linspace(0, Fs/2, length(X)/2+1);
% Plot the magnitude of the Fourier Transform
plot(f, abs(X(1:length(X)/2+1)));
xlabel('Frequency (Hz)');
ylabel('Magnitude');
```
In this example, we define a time variable `t` and a signal `x` that is a sum of two sine waves. We then compute the Fourier Transform of `x` using the `fft` function. Finally, we compute the frequency vector using the sampling frequency `Fs` and plot the magnitude of the Fourier Transform.

