Sure, I can help you with that. There are several sorting algorithms that can be used to sort a set of values in ascending order, such as Bubble Sort, Insertion Sort, Selection Sort, Merge Sort, Quick Sort, and Heap Sort. Each algorithm has its own time complexity and efficiency.
For this task, I suggest using Quick Sort, which has an average time complexity of O(n log n) and is one of the most efficient sorting algorithms for large sets of values.
Here's a Python code implementation of Quick Sort for the given set of values:
```
def quick_sort(arr):
    if len(arr) <= 1:
        return arr
    else:
        pivot = arr[0]
        left = []
        right = []
        for i in range(1, len(arr)):
            if arr[i] < pivot:
                left.append(arr[i])
            else:
                right.append(arr[i])
        return quick_sort(left) + [pivot] + quick_sort(right)
arr = [10, 11, 8, 12, 7]
sorted_arr = quick_sort(arr)
print(sorted_arr)
```
This code will output the sorted array in ascending order: `[7, 8, 10, 11, 12]`.
To analyze the time complexity of Quick Sort, we can use the big O notation. The worst-case time complexity of Quick Sort is O(n^2) when the pivot element is the smallest or largest element in the array, which can happen if the array is already sorted or nearly sorted. However, the average-case time complexity of Quick Sort is O(n log n) when the pivot element is the median of the array or close to it, which is the case in most scenarios. Therefore, Quick Sort is an efficient sorting algorithm for large sets of values.

